A nib says who owns a frame, and a custom view dropped its constraints.

Two decode gaps, both reached from the same measurement, and neither is application specific.

ONE. -[NSCustomView initWithCoder:] builds a different object by hand and never calls
[super initWithCoder:], so everything -[NSView initWithCoder:] decodes beyond the frame, the
vFlags, the tag, the subviews and the layer settings is lost. The expensive loss is
NSViewConstraints. A view laid out in Interface Builder has no autoresizing mask to fall back on,
so every subview stays at the frame IB last wrote, and IB writes overlapping frames freely because
constraints were going to move them.

TWO. -[NSView initWithCoder:] never decoded who owns the frame. The line read
_translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = YES with a TODO above it, so every nib view claimed
to own its own frame and constraints on a container could not move anything. The archive spells it
as a NEGATION, NSDoNotTranslateAutoresizingMask, so translates is the complement of that key.

MEASURED, with a control in the same run of the same application. Summing the constraint count over
every view, per window:

  iA Writer document window      212 constraints over 145 views     worked all along
  iA Writer Preferences window     0 constraints over  46 views     before
  iA Writer Preferences window    58 constraints over  46 views     after

58 is exactly the number of NSLayoutConstraint objects in GeneralPreferences.nib, and parsing that
nib says why they were lost: of its 550 objects exactly ONE carries NSViewConstraints, and its
class is NSCustomView. The translates key is carried by 43 objects in that nib and is true on every
one of them; the pane now reports 33 of 46 views owned by auto layout where it reported none.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX, said plainly. iA Writer Preferences still draws Appearance and Dock icon on
top of each other, two NSTextFields at the same y=417. The constraints are attached and the views
are owned, so the solver entry condition is met and it runs, and it still does not place them.
That is a third link and a much larger one: a solver able to satisfy these constraints. The two
gaps here are strictly more correct than before and are what makes that work reachable.

RISK, since this flips layout ownership for every nib view in every application and autoresizing
was at least approximating something before. Gates after it: dts 69 of 69, roster sweep 7 of 7 and
roster input 7 of 7 with BYTE IDENTICAL captures to the run before, and iA Writer and Swift
Publisher looked at individually because they are the two that lean hardest on nib layout.

CIDER_TRACE_CONSTRAINTS prints what the NSViewConstraints key hands back per view, which is how
"the key is never found" was told apart from "an empty array" and from "one object where an array
was expected".

--- a/AppKit/NSView.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSView.m
@@ -430,7 +430,37 @@
          * out in Interface Builder carries them here and has no autoresizing mask to fall back on,
          * so every subview of one stayed at the size it was archived with, usually 1x1.
          */
-        for (NSLayoutConstraint *constraint in [keyed decodeObjectForKey: @"NSViewConstraints"])
+        /* WHAT THE ARCHIVE HANDS BACK FOR THAT KEY, because a nib whose views all report cons=0
+         * while the file plainly contains NSLayoutConstraint objects could be a key that is never
+         * found, an empty array, or a single object where an array was expected. Those want
+         * different fixes. CIDER_TRACE_CONSTRAINTS. */
+        /*
+         * WHO OWNS THIS FRAME, which was a TODO above and defaulted to the mask every time.
+         *
+         * The archive spells it as a NEGATION: NSDoNotTranslateAutoresizingMask true means the
+         * view does NOT translate, so it is auto layout that places it. Defaulting every nib view
+         * to YES told this framework that each one owns its own frame, so constraints on the
+         * container could never move anything and every view stayed where Interface Builder last
+         * wrote it. IB writes overlapping frames freely, because constraints were going to sort
+         * them out, which is why iA Writer draws Appearance and Dock icon on top of each other.
+         *
+         * 43 of the 550 objects in that nib carry the key and every one of them is true.
+         */
+        if ([keyed containsValueForKey: @"NSDoNotTranslateAutoresizingMask"])
+            _translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints =
+                    ![keyed decodeBoolForKey: @"NSDoNotTranslateAutoresizingMask"];
+
+        id decodedConstraints = [keyed decodeObjectForKey: @"NSViewConstraints"];
+
+        if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_CONSTRAINTS") != NULL && decodedConstraints != nil) {
+            fprintf(stderr, "CIDER_CONSTRAINTS %s got %s count=%ld\n", object_getClassName(self),
+                    object_getClassName(decodedConstraints),
+                    [decodedConstraints respondsToSelector: @selector(count)]
+                            ? (long) [decodedConstraints count] : -1L);
+            fflush(stderr);
+        }
+
+        for (NSLayoutConstraint *constraint in decodedConstraints)
             [self addConstraint: constraint];
 
         // TODO: NSViewLayoutGuides, ...
